IMD gathers measurements from thousands of places. Automatic weather stations record temperature, rain, wind and [[humidity|humidity]]. Twice a day, from dozens of stations, staff release [[@weather-balloons|weather balloons]] carrying small instruments that radio back readings as they rise.

Doppler radars scan for rain and storms within a few hundred kilometres. Indian [[@weather-satellites|weather satellites]], such as INSAT-3DR, photograph clouds over the whole country and the oceans around it. All this data goes into [[@supercomputers|supercomputers]], which run models of the [[atmosphere|atmosphere]] to work out what will happen next.

{{story:weather-forecasts}}

Earth and Mars line up well for a trip only about every 26 months. Even then, [[@maths-speed-time|a journey takes about six to nine months]] each way. A crew would have to stay on Mars for many months before the planets lined up again for the trip home. A round trip could last around two and a half to three years.

During that time, astronauts would face radiation, weightlessness during the cruise and the strain of living in a tiny space far from home. Research on the [[@space-station|International Space Station]] shows that months in [[@space-body|space affect the body]], weakening bones and muscles and even changing eyesight.

{{story:space-body}}

In Kenya in the 1970s, the scientists Robert Seyfarth and Dorothy Cheney recorded vervet monkeys. They found the monkeys made different alarm calls for different [[predator|predators]]. A leopard call sent monkeys up into trees. An eagle call made them look up and rush into bushes. A snake call made them stand up and peer at the ground. When the scientists played the recordings from a hidden speaker, the monkeys reacted the same way, even with no predator in sight.

In Indian forests, other animals eavesdrop. Langurs watch from the treetops and give loud alarm barks when they spot a tiger or leopard. [[@deer-india|Chital deer]] below listen and run. In return, the deer's alarm calls warn [[@monkeys-langur|langurs]] on the ground.

{{story:whale-songs}}

Dust ruins paint, so the paint shop is one of the cleanest places in the factory. First, the whole body is dipped into a tank of liquid and charged with electricity, so a protective coat sticks to every corner, inside and out. This stops rust.

Robot arms then spray primer, colour and a clear top coat. All those layers together are usually about a tenth of a millimetre thick, roughly the thickness of a sheet of paper. The body bakes in ovens between coats, like a cake.

{{story:industrial-revolution}}

Scientists call the amount of help a machine gives its *mechanical advantage*. A [[@lever-lift|lever]] gets it by having a long arm and a short arm. A set of pulleys gets it by sharing the weight between many strands of rope.

Here's the trade in numbers. Say you want to lift a 100 kg crate onto a truck, 1 metre up. With a single fixed pulley you pull 1 metre of rope with the force of 100 kg, more than most adults can manage. With a set of pulleys that gives four strands, you pull with the force of about 25 kg, but you pull 4 metres of rope. The same work gets done, just spread over a longer distance. A little extra is always lost to [[@friction|friction]] in the wheels.

{{story:lever-lift}}

गरबा आम तौर पर धीरे शुरू होता है, सीधे कदमों और दो-तीन तालियों के साथ। जैसे-जैसे ढोल और गायक रफ़्तार बढ़ाते हैं, नाचने वाले घुमाव, झुकाव और तेज़ कदम जोड़ते जाते हैं। मशहूर कदमों के नाम हैं *दो ताली* और *तीन ताली*।

ताल ढोल और गायकों से आती है, अक्सर हारमोनियम के साथ, और अब कीबोर्ड और इलेक्ट्रॉनिक संगीत के साथ भी। गुजरात में हज़ारों की भीड़ के लिए लाइव बैंड बजते हैं। सबके साथ कदम मिलाकर चलना सीखना ही आधा मज़ा है। यह [[@rhythm-beat|ताल]] का वही खिंचाव है जो किसी गाने पर तुम्हारे पैर थिरका देता है।

{{story:durga-puja}}

Jobs come and go as technology changes. Before alarm clocks were common, "knocker-uppers" in British industrial towns tapped on windows with long poles to wake factory workers. Lamplighters lit gas streetlamps every evening. Telephone operators connected calls by plugging wires into boards. Most of these jobs are gone.

Once, "computer" meant a person. At NASA, [[@moon-race-women|human computers]] like [[@usa-nasa-hidden-figures|Katherine Johnson]] calculated flight paths by hand. When electronic computers arrived, some of these women became early programmers.

{{story:women-maths}}

In the [[@world-war-two|Second World War]], German forces used the Enigma machine. It looked like a typewriter, with spinning wheels inside that changed the cipher with every key pressed. The number of possible settings was so vast that guessing was hopeless.

At Bletchley Park in England, thousands of people worked to break it. Among them was the mathematician [[@alan-turing|Alan Turing]], who helped design machines called bombes to test settings quickly. They built on earlier work by Polish mathematicians. Historians believe the decoded messages helped the Allies win sooner.

{{story:alan-turing}}

The Olympics split climbing into three kinds. In bouldering, climbers tackle short walls about 4 metres high with no ropes, only thick crash mats below. Each "problem" is a puzzle of a few hard moves. In lead climbing, climbers clip a rope as they go up a tall wall and try to get as high as possible in six minutes.

Speed climbing is a straight race. Two climbers go side by side up identical 15-metre walls that lean out at 5 degrees. The holds are always in the same places, all over the world, so climbers can practise the exact route. In 2026, China's Zhao Yicheng set the men's record at 4.54 seconds. America's Emma Hunt set the women's record at 5.99 seconds. That's faster than most people can climb a ladder one storey high.

{{story:power-gecko-feet}}

Khashaba Dadasaheb Jadhav came from Goleshwar, a village in Satara district, Maharashtra. He was small, quick and very fit. In 1952 he qualified for the Helsinki Olympics, but needed money for the journey. People in his village and region raised funds, and the principal of his old college reportedly mortgaged his house to help.

In Helsinki he won the bronze medal in bantamweight freestyle wrestling. For 44 years he was India's only individual Olympic medallist, until the tennis player Leander Paes won bronze in 1996. Jadhav never received a major national sports award in his lifetime. In 2001, long after his death, he was given the Arjuna Award.

{{story:muscles}}

गरमी के दिन त्वचा से [[@sweat|पसीना]] निकलता है। जब वह भाप बनकर उड़ता है, तो अपने साथ गरमी ले जाता है। त्वचा की खून की नलियाँ भी चौड़ी हो जाती हैं, ताकि ज़्यादा गरम खून सतह के पास बहकर गरमी छोड़ सके। इसीलिए दौड़ने के बाद चेहरा लाल हो जाता है।

ठंड लगने पर यही नलियाँ सिकुड़ जाती हैं, ताकि गरमी अंदर बनी रहे। नन्ही-नन्ही मांसपेशियाँ बालों को सीधा खड़ा कर देती हैं, और तुम्हारे [[@goosebumps|रोंगटे खड़े]] हो जाते हैं।

{{story:octopus-smart}}

ज़मीन तक पहुँचने वाली UV मुख्य रूप से दो तरह की होती है। UVB किरणें छोटी होती हैं और ज़्यादातर जलाने का काम यही करती हैं। UVA किरणें लंबी होती हैं, त्वचा में और गहरे तक जाती हैं, और झुर्रियों और लंबे समय के नुकसान को बढ़ाती हैं। UVA तो खिड़की के काँच के पार भी निकल जाती है।

ज़्यादातर UV आसमान में बहुत ऊपर [[@ozone-layer|ओज़ोन परत]] रोक लेती है। फिर भी इतनी पहुँच जाती है कि फ़र्क पड़े, ख़ासकर भूमध्य रेखा के पास, ऊँचे पहाड़ों पर और दोपहर के आस-पास। भारत के ज़्यादातर हिस्सों में साल के कई महीने बहुत तेज़ UV पहुँचती है।

{{story:ozone-layer}}

Most houses were built of sun-dried mud brick around an open courtyard, which let in light and air. Rooms were sparsely furnished, with stools, chests and couches.

People wore a simple tunic called a chiton, made from a rectangle of wool or linen pinned at the shoulders. A cloak went over the top in cold weather. Wealthy men held drinking parties called symposia, where guests lay on couches, sang, played games and argued about ideas. Painted [[@pottery|pottery]] shows these scenes in such detail that we can almost hear the music.

{{story:olympics}}

The oldest Chinese writing that experts are sure about comes from the Shang [[dynasty|dynasty]], around 1250 BCE. Kings asked questions about the harvest, the weather and wars, and priests carved them onto turtle shells and the shoulder bones of oxen. They then heated the bones until they cracked and read the answer in the cracks.

For centuries, farmers dug up these bones and sold them to medicine shops as "dragon bones". In 1899, a scholar noticed ancient writing on them. Thousands have since been found near Anyang, in northern China. Many of their characters are ancestors of ones still used today, which makes Chinese one of the oldest writing systems still in use.

{{story:calligraphy}}

In [[@greek-myths|Greek myths]], sirens sang so sweetly that sailors steered onto rocks. In Homer's Odyssey, the hero Odysseus plugs his crew's ears with wax and has himself tied to the mast so he can hear the song safely.

But the oldest Greek sirens weren't fish. Vase paintings show them as birds with women's heads. In [[medieval|medieval]] Europe, writers mixed sirens up with sea creatures, and they gained tails. In French, Spanish and Italian, the word for mermaid still comes from "siren".

{{story:marine-parks-india}}

भारत की कई परंपराओं में पीला शुभ और ख़ुशी का रंग है। हिंदू शादी से पहले हल्दी की रस्म होती है, जिसमें दोस्त और घरवाले हँसते-गाते दूल्हा-दुल्हन पर हल्दी का लेप मलते हैं।

{{story:weddings-india}}

हल्दी भारत के सबसे पुराने रंगों में से भी है। इससे [[@clothes-cotton|सूती]] और रेशमी कपड़े चटक पीले हो जाते थे, हालाँकि धूप में यह रंग जल्दी उड़ जाता था। रंगरेज़ अक्सर इसे दूसरे पौधों के रंगों के साथ मिलाकर हरा और नारंगी बनाते थे। यह हुनर तुम [[@block-printing|ठप्पा छपाई]] में देख सकते हो।
